TERM SHEET SUNDAY - MIDWEEK EXTRA
Wednesday, March 18, 2026

A short one because the news is worth the interruption.

Cobalt Ridge Partners closed its fourth fund this morning at $900M, above its $750M target and nearly double fund III. Cobalt Ridge is the largest of the specialist funds that invest only in industrial and supply-chain software, and until about two years ago the generalists treated the category as a backwater. The oversubscription tells you the limited partners no longer agree.

Three things this means for founders in the category.

First, there is more Series A and B capital aimed specifically at plant-floor, logistics and industrial-AI companies than at any point in the history of the sector. Cobalt Ridge alone will write 25-30 checks from this fund; the three next-largest specialists have all raised in the last eighteen months.

Second, the generalists will follow. They always do. Expect the large multi-stage funds to announce "industrial AI" theses and hire partners from the specialists by summer. Their checks will be larger and their processes slower, and they will want to see a specialist in the round with them.

Third, valuations in the category will drift up, at least for the companies with reference customers and a live deployment. The specialists know what a plant rollout costs and price it; the generalists will pay for the story. Founders should take the specialist's money and the generalist's valuation, in that order, if they can get both.

The fund's announcement lists its first three investments from fund IV, all Series B extensions into existing portfolio companies, which is how specialists tend to start a fund. New Series A activity from it will show up in Q2.

Back to the regular schedule Sunday.

